{"scope":{"contains":"Determinations this service RECORDED: an attestation arrived on a payment request, was ingested, and its document bytes were stored. Each row carries the document, the signature and its OWN `construction` — the one that row was checked with, which is `none` for a row this service did not verify. The file-level `construction` below describes the check this service performs; it is not a promise that every row went through it, and a row saying `none` did not.","excludes":["Determinations that never reached this service. Nothing here observes a decider directly.","Determinations recorded before determination records existed. Payments predating this record type carry a derived attestation block inside the payment record and no document, so they cannot be exported as verifiable rows and are not counted as though they could.","Payments that cited nothing. No document arrived, so there is no determination — see `enforcementWithoutDetermination` for how many enforcement actions that covers.","Determinations reaching this service other than on a payment request. That entry point (R5) does not exist yet, so today this is empty rather than merely unlisted."],"completeness":"NOT ASSERTED, AND NOT ASSERTABLE HERE. This file does not prove that nothing was omitted between any two points in time. Nothing in this architecture provides that property: there is no counter a reader can reconcile these rows against, and the absence of a row is not evidence that no determination occurred. Do not compute a denominator from this file and do not read a gap in it as an absence of decisions."},"policyRefConvention":{"status":"convention, not enforced. Nothing is refused for omitting any of this.","placement":"Put policy locators INSIDE `policyRef`. Fields nested there are carried whole into the verified block, because the engine copies the sub-object by reference. Fields added at the TOP LEVEL of the attestation are signed and verified and then DROPPED from the verified block — this service recovers them into `documentOnly`, so they are no longer lost here, but no other consumer of the engine will see them. Inside `policyRef` is the placement that works everywhere.","fields":{"clauses":"The clause locators the decider actually read, in the LAW'S OWN addressing scheme rather than ours — \"III.4.e\", not an offset into a file we chose. CAPTURED AT ISSUE TIME OR NEVER: a date selects clauses rather than documents, so nothing downstream can reconstruct which clauses were before the decider. This is the field with a deadline.","version":"The policy version in the publisher's own scheme. `vocabularyRef` requires this and states why: artifacts under different versions are not comparable, so a version that is not stated cannot be compared against. A version encoded inside an opaque `id` — a trailing \"/v1\" — cannot be compared by anything.","publisherId":"The document's own identifiers in the publisher's scheme, e.g. \"85 FR 36308\". This is what a reader cites and what a librarian resolves; our `id` and `hash` are neither.","retrievedFrom":"Where the decider actually fetched the bytes it hashed. `id` is an identifier and this system never dereferences it — see `policyRefIdResolvability` on every row. A retrieval coordinate is the decider's fact, which is why it is asked for rather than derived."},"limits":"None of this makes `policyRef` able to answer \"what was the law on a date\". A single hash identifies bytes that were read; an amending instrument revises some clauses and leaves others standing, so a date selects clauses rather than documents and no single artifact is \"the policy as of\" anything. These fields make \"what did you read\" answerable and precise. They do not make the other question answerable, and a surface must not present them as though they did."},"construction":{"id":"jcs-2022-over-parsed-document","algorithm":"ed25519","signatureEncoding":"base64","documentEncoding":"base64","steps":["Decode `document` from base64 to bytes. These are the bytes as this service received them.","Parse the bytes as JSON.","Canonicalise the parsed value: object keys sorted by UTF-16 code unit, no insignificant whitespace, strings escaped as JSON. This is RFC 8785 restricted to the domain below.","Verify `signature` over the canonical bytes with ed25519, against the key recovered from `decider` (a did:key carries its own ed25519 key; a did:web requires resolving its DID document)."],"domain":{"covers":["string","array","object"],"refuses":["number","boolean","null"],"note":"The signing side REFUSES to issue an attestation containing a number, boolean or null, so a document that could make this construction and general RFC 8785 disagree cannot exist. That restriction is why an off-the-shelf RFC 8785 implementation reproduces these bytes. It is stated because a holder who does not know it cannot tell whether their library agreeing with us is a property or a coincidence."},"integrity":"The `document` field is the byte sequence this service received, copied and never reparsed, reserialised or reformatted. VERIFY THE SIGNATURE AGAINST IT DIRECTLY — but note what that does and does not establish: this construction canonicalises before checking, so a document whose bytes were reshaped (reindented, keys reordered, reserialised) STILL VERIFIES. The signature binds the decider to the VALUES, not to these exact bytes. To establish that the bytes are the ones this service received, recompute `documentHash` over them; that is the check which moves when the bytes move."},"window":{"since":null,"until":null,"basis":"observedAt","timezone":null,"unplaceable":0,"unplaceableNote":"Records carrying no `observedAt` cannot be placed against a window on this service's clock. They are counted here and NOT backfilled: an observedAt invented after the fact would assert that this service observed something it did not.","excluded":0,"total":12,"count":12},"enforcementWithoutDetermination":{"count":0,"byDisposition":{}},"dete